---
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const title = "Canadian Experience Class (CEC) 2026: Requirements, Draws and the New CRS Points";
const description = "Canadian Experience Class in late 2026: the 12-month work rule, CLB by TEER, fees, 2026 CEC draws with cutoffs of 507-523, and what the new CRS points change.";
const canonical = "https://bcpnpcalculator.ca/articles/canadian-experience-class";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What are the Canadian Experience Class requirements?",
    a: "You need at least 1 year (1,560 hours) of paid, skilled work in Canada in TEER 0, 1, 2 or 3 occupations in the 3 years before you apply, gained while you were authorized to work as a temporary resident. You also need an approved language test showing CLB 7 in all four abilities if your work was in TEER 0 or 1, or CLB 5 if it was in TEER 2 or 3, and you must plan to live outside Quebec. There is no education requirement, and IRCC's eligibility page does not ask for proof of funds.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does part-time work count for the Canadian Experience Class?",
    a: "Yes. IRCC counts 1 year of full-time work at 30 hours a week for 12 months, or the same total of 1,560 hours through part-time work, for example 15 hours a week for 24 months. You can combine several jobs. Hours above 30 a week do not get you there faster, and work done while you were a full-time student, including co-op terms, does not count.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does self-employment count toward CEC?",
    a: "No. IRCC's eligibility page lists self-employment as work that does not count, with an exception only for some physicians under a temporary public policy. Volunteer work, unpaid internships and any work you were not authorized to do in Canada are also excluded.",
  },
  {
    q: "What was the last CEC draw?",
    a: "As of November 15, 2026, the most recent Canadian Experience Class round was round 449 on October 15, 2026: 2,500 invitations with a cutoff of 519. IRCC paused all invitation rounds on November 3 while it recalculates every profile under the new CRS rules and had not held a round since October 28 when this was written.",
  },
  {
    q: "How long does CEC processing take?",
    a: "IRCC's processing time tool showed about 6 months for Canadian Experience Class applications, with about 53,900 people waiting, in its estimate last updated October 9, 2026. That clock starts when you submit your permanent residence application after an invitation, not when you enter the pool.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is CEC better than the Federal Skilled Worker Program?",
    a: "If you qualify for both, you do not have to choose: your Express Entry profile is considered for every program you are eligible for. CEC is easier to meet because it has no education requirement, no 67-point grid and no proof of funds, and because it has had its own invitation rounds through 2026. FSW suits people whose skilled experience is mostly outside Canada.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I keep working in Canada while my CEC application is processed?",
    a: "Often, yes. A Canadian Experience Class applicant who is in Canada with valid work authorization (or maintained or restorable status) and whose permanent residence application has passed IRCC's completeness check can apply for a bridging open work permit. IRCC's fee list shows $155 for the work permit plus the $100 open work permit holder fee.",
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": faqs.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": f.q,
    "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": f.a },
  })),
};
