      </div>
    </header>

    <figure class="mb-10">
      <img src="/images/articles/canadian-experience-class.png" width="1200" height="630" alt="A 36-month timeline with 12 months highlighted in green, showing that the Canadian Experience Class needs 1 year or 1,560 hours of skilled Canadian work within the last 3 years, with CLB 7 for TEER 0 and 1 jobs and CLB 5 for TEER 2 and 3 jobs."
           class="w-full h-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
    </figure>

    <div class="bg-emerald-50 border-l-4 border-emerald-500 p-6 mb-10 rounded-r-xl">
      <p class="font-semibold text-emerald-900 mb-2">Quick answer</p>
      <p class="text-emerald-900">
        The Canadian Experience Class is the Express Entry program for skilled workers who already have Canadian work experience. You need at least 1 year (1,560 hours) of paid work in TEER 0, 1, 2 or 3 jobs in Canada in the 3 years before you apply, done with work authorization, plus CLB 7 (TEER 0 or 1) or CLB 5 (TEER 2 or 3) in all four language abilities. There is no education or proof-of-funds requirement. In 2026, IRCC held 18 CEC rounds with 57,750 invitations and cutoffs of 507 to 523, and since November 3 Canadian experience in a high-wage occupation can add up to 70 extra CRS points.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-emerald-600 mb-2">1,560 h</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Skilled Canadian work needed in the last 3 years (1 year at 30 h/week)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-emerald-600 mb-2">18</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">CEC rounds in 2026 to November 15, issuing 57,750 invitations</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-emerald-600 mb-2">507&ndash;523</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Range of 2026 CEC cutoffs, all under the pre-November 3 CRS grid</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <nav class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-10" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">In this guide</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-1 text-blue-700">
        <li><a href="#requirements" class="hover:underline">Canadian Experience Class requirements</a></li>
        <li><a href="#draws" class="hover:underline">CEC draws in 2026</a></li>
        <li><a href="#crs-changes" class="hover:underline">How the November 3, 2026 CRS changes affect CEC candidates</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pgwp" class="hover:underline">From PGWP to CEC: timing it right</a></li>
        <li><a href="#fees" class="hover:underline">Fees, processing time and working while you wait</a></li>
        <li><a href="#cec-vs-fsw" class="hover:underline">CEC vs the Federal Skilled Worker Program</a></li>
        <li><a href="#bc" class="hover:underline">Options for workers in B.C.</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq" class="hover:underline">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        The Canadian Experience Class (CEC) is one of the three federal programs managed through Express Entry, alongside the Federal Skilled Worker and Federal Skilled Trades programs. It exists for people who have already proved themselves in the Canadian labour market: former international students on post-graduation work permits, employer-sponsored workers, spouses with open work permits and working holiday participants. Meeting the rules gets you into the Express Entry pool; an invitation still depends on your Comprehensive Ranking System (CRS) score and on which rounds IRCC chooses to hold.
      </p>

      <h2 id="requirements" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Canadian Experience Class Requirements in 2026</h2>
      <p>
        IRCC's CEC eligibility page (page details dated October 16, 2026) sets out four groups of requirements: skilled Canadian work experience, language, admissibility and a plan to live outside Quebec.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">1. At least 1 year of skilled work in Canada</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Amount:</strong> at least 1 year of work, or 1,560 hours in total, in the 3 years before you apply. Full-time means 30 hours a week for 12 months. Part-time work counts if it adds up to the same hours, for example 15 hours a week for 24 months, and you can combine several jobs.</li>
        <li><strong>Skill level:</strong> the work must be in one or more NOC 2021 occupations in TEER 0, 1, 2 or 3, and your duties must match the occupation's lead statement and most of its main duties. Job title alone is not enough.</li>
        <li><strong>Status:</strong> the work must be paid and done in Canada while you held temporary resident status with authorization to work. Remote work for a Canadian employer counts for CRS purposes if you were physically in Canada.</li>
        <li><strong>What does not count:</strong> work done during full-time study (including co-op terms), self-employment (except some physicians under a temporary public policy), volunteer work, unpaid internships, and any work you were not authorized to do.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because the 3-year window looks back from the date you apply, older experience slowly drops off. If your qualifying year is near the edge of the window, an invitation that comes late can leave you short on the day you submit your application, so keep a running tally of dates and hours from pay stubs and reference letters.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">2. Language: CLB depends on your TEER</h3>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">TEER of your Canadian work</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">English minimum</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">French minimum</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Examples</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">TEER 0 or 1</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CLB 7 in all four abilities</td><td class="px-4 py-3">NCLC 7</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Managers, software engineers, accountants, registered nurses</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">TEER 2 or 3</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CLB 5 in all four abilities</td><td class="px-4 py-3">NCLC 5</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Electricians, cooks, technicians, administrative assistants</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        You must take one of IRCC's approved English or French tests, and the results must be less than 2 years old both when you create your profile and when you submit your permanent residence application. The minimum only makes you eligible: higher scores earn far more CRS points than any other single factor most candidates can change quickly.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">3. No education requirement, but education still pays</h3>
      <p>
        CEC has no minimum education. A Canadian credential, or a completed foreign credential with an educational credential assessment (ECA), still earns CRS points under education and under skill transferability, where education combined with 2 or more years of Canadian work experience is worth up to 50 points. A Canadian post-secondary credential also adds 15 or 30 additional points.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">4. No proof of funds</h3>
      <p>
        IRCC's CEC eligibility page does not list a settlement funds requirement, and the Express Entry proof-of-funds rules apply to Federal Skilled Worker and Federal Skilled Trades candidates. If you are also eligible for FSW, check how your profile is assessed before you assume you are exempt.
      </p>

      <h2 id="draws" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">CEC Draws in 2026: Rounds, Invitations and Cutoffs</h2>
      <p>
        IRCC published 18 Canadian Experience Class rounds between January 7 and October 15, 2026, according to its rounds of invitations data (checked November 15, 2026). Together they issued <strong>57,750 invitations</strong>, with cutoffs between <strong>507 and 523</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Period</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">CEC rounds</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Invitations</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Cutoffs</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">January to March</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6</td><td class="px-4 py-3">30,250</td><td class="px-4 py-3">507 to 511</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">April to June</td><td class="px-4 py-3">4</td><td class="px-4 py-3">11,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">514 to 518</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">July to October 15</td><td class="px-4 py-3">8</td><td class="px-4 py-3">16,500</td><td class="px-4 py-3">516 to 523</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-emerald-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">2026 total</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">18</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">57,750</td><td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold">507 to 523</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The pattern was clear. Early-year rounds were large (8,000 on January 7 and 6,000 on both January 21 and February 17), and the lowest cutoff, 507, came on March 17. From late spring, rounds shrank to between 1,000 and 4,000 invitations and cutoffs rose: the smallest round, 1,000 invitations on August 18, had the highest cutoff of the year at 523. The most recent CEC round was <strong>round 449 on October 15, 2026: 2,500 invitations at 519</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        No round of any type has been held since October 28. IRCC paused invitations on November 3 while it recalculates every profile under the new CRS rules, a process it said could take up to two weeks, and as of November 15 it had not announced when rounds resume. Our <a href="/articles/express-entry-draws" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry draws tracker</a> lists every 2026 round and will show the first post-pause results.
      </p>

      <h2 id="crs-changes" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How the November 3, 2026 CRS Changes Affect CEC Candidates</h2>
      <p>
        On November 3, 2026, IRCC replaced the old core "Canadian work experience" row with a new <strong>Labour Market Integration factor</strong>. It gives points for either Canadian work experience or a high-wage job offer, but not both. For most CEC candidates this is the most important change to the CRS in years.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Canadian work experience</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Without spouse</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">With spouse</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">1 year</td><td class="px-4 py-3">40</td><td class="px-4 py-3">35</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">2 years</td><td class="px-4 py-3">53</td><td class="px-4 py-3">46</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">3 years</td><td class="px-4 py-3">64</td><td class="px-4 py-3">56</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">4 years</td><td class="px-4 py-3">72</td><td class="px-4 py-3">63</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">5 years or more</td><td class="px-4 py-3">80</td><td class="px-4 py-3">70</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-emerald-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Plus: at least 12 months in a high-wage occupation</td><td class="px-4 py-3" colspan="2">70 (median wage at least 2x the national median), 40 (1.5x) or 20 (1.3x)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The factor is capped at 150 points without a spouse or partner (80 plus 70) and 140 with one (70 plus 70). "High-wage" refers to the occupation's median wage, not your own pay: IRCC's October 16, 2026 announcement says an occupation qualifies if its median wage is at least 1.3 times the median for the whole Canadian workforce, using Job Bank, Labour Force Survey and Census data from the previous three years, and that it keeps the list of occupations on its website. Health care, skilled trades, engineering, technology and education are named as examples.
      </p>
      <p>What this means in practice:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>High-wage CEC candidates gain the most.</strong> A single candidate with 1 year of Canadian work in an occupation at twice the national median now gets 40 plus 70, or 110 points from this factor. The same candidate in an occupation below the 1.3x threshold gets 40.</li>
        <li><strong>CEC candidates in other TEER 0 to 3 jobs keep their length-of-work points</strong> but gain nothing extra, so they may slip in the rankings relative to high-wage peers.</li>
        <li><strong>Job offer points now follow the occupation's wage, not an LMIA.</strong> Since November 3, a qualifying job offer does not need an LMIA, the grid no longer lists job offer points under additional points, and offer points (100, 70 or 50) go only to offers in high-wage occupations. Candidates who relied on job offer points under the old grid may drop.</li>
        <li><strong>Old cutoffs are no longer a guide.</strong> All 2026 CEC cutoffs were set under the old grid. Expect the first rounds after the pause to look different, and do not assume a 519 under the new rules means what it meant in October.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        IRCC said profiles already in the pool are rescored automatically, including Canadian work experience points; candidates had to add job offer details themselves before November 3 to receive job offer points. Check your work history entries, NOC codes and dates in your <a href="/articles/express-entry-profile" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry profile</a>, then estimate your new total with our <a href="/articles/express-entry-crs-score-calculator" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">CRS score calculator guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="pgwp" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">From PGWP to CEC: Timing It Right</h2>
      <p>
        For international graduates the usual path is study, then a post-graduation work permit (PGWP), then CEC. A PGWP lasts at most the length of your program for programs under 2 years, or up to 3 years for programs of 2 years or more and for eligible master's degrees, and IRCC says you cannot get a second PGWP. That makes the clock tight.
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Months 0 to 12:</strong> get into a TEER 0 to 3 job as soon as possible. Work you did while you were a full-time student does not count. Book your language test toward the end of this period so the results stay valid longer.</li>
        <li><strong>At 1,560 hours:</strong> create your profile as soon as you have the full year. If you are close to a higher TEER language score, retaking the test can matter more than waiting.</li>
        <li><strong>While in the pool:</strong> keep working. A second year adds 13 work-length points (53 instead of 40 for a single candidate) and can double some skill transferability points, for example from 25 to 50 for a master's degree holder.</li>
        <li><strong>After an invitation:</strong> submit your permanent residence application and, if your permit is close to expiring, apply for a bridging open work permit once the application passes the completeness check.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        If you hold a 1-year PGWP, you reach the CEC minimum only at the very end of your permit even if you start work on day one, and part-time work stretches that further. Plan a back-up, such as a provincial nomination or an employer-specific work permit, early.
      </p>

      <h2 id="fees" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">CEC Fees, Processing Time and Working While You Wait</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Item</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Fee (CAD)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Principal applicant (processing fee $990 + right of permanent residence fee $600)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$1,590</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Spouse or common-law partner</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$1,590</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Dependent child, each</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$270</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Biometrics</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$85 per person, $170 family maximum</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Bridging open work permit (work permit + open work permit holder fee)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$155 + $100</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Fees are from IRCC's fee list as checked November 15, 2026 (page modified November 10, 2026). The right of permanent residence fee is the only fee IRCC can refund after processing starts. Budget separately for language tests, police certificates, a medical exam and, if you want education points for foreign study, an ECA.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Processing time:</strong> IRCC's tool showed <strong>about 6 months</strong> for CEC, with about 53,900 people waiting, in its estimate last updated October 9, 2026. For comparison it showed about 7 months for both Federal Skilled Worker and Express Entry provincial nominee applications. See our <a href="/articles/ircc-processing-times-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">IRCC processing times guide</a> for how these estimates work.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Working while you wait:</strong> IRCC's instructions let a CEC applicant in Canada apply for a bridging open work permit if they hold valid work authorization (or have maintained or restorable status) and their electronic permanent residence application has passed the completeness check. If the application turns out to be incomplete, the open work permit holder fee is refunded but the processing fee is not. Our <a href="/articles/bridging-open-work-permit-bowp" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">bridging open work permit guide</a> covers the steps.
      </p>

      <h2 id="cec-vs-fsw" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Canadian Experience Class vs Federal Skilled Worker</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Rule</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Canadian Experience Class</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left">Federal Skilled Worker</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Work experience</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1 year in Canada in the last 3 years, TEER 0 to 3</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1 year continuous in the last 10 years, anywhere, TEER 0 to 3</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Language</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CLB 7 or CLB 5 depending on TEER</td><td class="px-4 py-3">CLB 7</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Education</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Not required</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Required (with ECA for foreign credentials)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Points grid</td><td class="px-4 py-3">None</td><td class="px-4 py-3">67 out of 100</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Proof of funds</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Not listed</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Required unless exempt</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Program-specific rounds in 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">18</td><td class="px-4 py-3">None</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        You do not apply to one or the other: Express Entry checks your profile against every program you meet. For details of the FSW grid and funds, see our <a href="/articles/federal-skilled-worker-program" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Federal Skilled Worker Program guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bc" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Options for CEC Candidates Working in B.C.</h2>
      <p>
        If your CRS score sits below recent CEC cutoffs, a provincial nomination is the most reliable boost: it adds 600 points. B.C. workers with an eligible job offer can register with the BC PNP and, if they are also in the Express Entry pool, pursue the Express Entry BC option, which leads to an enhanced nomination. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-express-entry-bc-eebc-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry BC guide</a> explains how it works.
      </p>
      <p>
        In 2026 the BC PNP has invited mainly through targeted draws for child care, health, veterinary care and construction, and through Innovate draws based on wage or points. The November 13, 2026 targeted draw, for example, invited 128 construction workers at a minimum score of 94 and 103 health workers at 72. See the latest results on our <a href="/bc-pnp-draws" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP draws page</a> and estimate your registration score with the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-emerald-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a>.
      </p>

